{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"DOI:10.1016/j.celrep.2015.12.080","resultType":"core","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":25,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"26776524","source":"MED","pmid":"26776524","pmcid":"PMC4740242","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC4740242"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.celrep.2015.12.080","title":"Kinase Inhibitor Profiling Reveals Unexpected Opportunities to Inhibit Disease-Associated Mutant Kinases.","authorString":"Duong-Ly KC, Devarajan K, Liang S, Horiuchi KY, Wang Y, Ma H, Peterson JR.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Duong-Ly KC","firstName":"Krisna C","lastName":"Duong-Ly","initials":"KC","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4593-8145"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Program in Cancer Biology, Fox Chase Cancer Center, 333 Cottman Avenue, Philadelphia, PA 19111, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Devarajan K","firstName":"Karthik","lastName":"Devarajan","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biostatistics and Bioinformatics, Fox Chase Cancer Center, 333 Cottman Avenue, Philadelphia, PA 19111, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Liang S","firstName":"Shuguang","lastName":"Liang","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Reaction Biology Corporation, 1 Great Valley Parkway, Suite 2, Malvern, PA 19355, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Horiuchi KY","firstName":"Kurumi Y","lastName":"Horiuchi","initials":"KY","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Reaction Biology Corporation, 1 Great Valley Parkway, Suite 2, Malvern, PA 19355, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang Y","firstName":"Yuren","lastName":"Wang","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Reaction Biology Corporation, 1 Great Valley Parkway, Suite 2, Malvern, PA 19355, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Ma H","firstName":"Haiching","lastName":"Ma","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Reaction Biology Corporation, 1 Great Valley Parkway, Suite 2, Malvern, PA 19355, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Peterson JR","firstName":"Jeffrey R","lastName":"Peterson","initials":"JR","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0604-718X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Program in Cancer Biology, Fox Chase Cancer Center, 333 Cottman Avenue, Philadelphia, PA 19111, USA. Electronic address: jeffrey.peterson@fccc.edu."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0604-718X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4593-8145"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data","recommended_reviews"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"4","volume":"14","journalIssueId":2371262,"dateOfPublication":"2016 Feb","monthOfPublication":2,"yearOfPublication":2016,"printPublicationDate":"2016-02-01","journal":{"title":"Cell reports","medlineAbbreviation":"Cell Rep","isoabbreviation":"Cell Rep","nlmid":"101573691","essn":"2211-1247","issn":"2211-1247"}},"pubYear":"2016","pageInfo":"772-781","abstractText":"Small-molecule kinase inhibitors have typically been designed to inhibit wild-type kinases rather than the mutant forms that frequently arise in diseases such as cancer. Mutations can have serious clinical implications by increasing kinase catalytic activity or conferring therapeutic resistance. To identify opportunities to repurpose inhibitors against disease-associated mutant kinases, we conducted a large-scale functional screen of 183 known kinase inhibitors against 76 recombinant mutant kinases. The results revealed lead compounds with activity against clinically important mutant kinases, including ALK, LRRK2, RET, and EGFR, as well as unexpected opportunities for repurposing FDA-approved kinase inhibitors as leads for additional indications. Furthermore, using T674I PDGFRα as an example, we show how single-dose screening data can provide predictive structure-activity data to guide subsequent inhibitor optimization. This study provides a resource for the development of inhibitors against numerous disease-associated mutant kinases and illustrates the potential of unbiased profiling as an approach to compound-centric inhibitor development.","affiliation":"Program in Cancer Biology, Fox Chase Cancer Center, 333 Cottman Avenue, Philadelphia, PA 19111, USA.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Journal Article","Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"T32 CA009035","agency":"NCI NIH HHS","acronym":"CA","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"R01 GM083025","agency":"NIGMS NIH HHS","acronym":"GM","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"P30 CA006927","agency":"NCI NIH HHS","acronym":"CA","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cell Line, Tumor"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Receptor, Platelet-Derived Growth Factor alpha","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"AI","qualifierName":"antagonists & inhibitors","majorTopic_YN":"Y"},{"abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Antineoplastic Agents","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"PD","qualifierName":"pharmacology","majorTopic_YN":"Y"},{"abbreviation":"CH","qualifierName":"chemistry","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Protein Kinase Inhibitors","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"PD","qualifierName":"pharmacology","majorTopic_YN":"Y"},{"abbreviation":"CH","qualifierName":"chemistry","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Protein Binding"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Mutation"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName